<div class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Introduction</p>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A SUI view is two files: a <code>.sui</code> template and a paired
    controller. The compiler turns them into a small, fast web component — no
    bundler runtime, no virtual DOM.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.install}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Your first view</h2>
  <p>
    The template declares the markup and binds to scope. The controller owns the
    state. Reading <code>name()</code> in the template subscribes that text node
    to the signal.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.template}></sui-codeblock>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.controller}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>The two-file model</h2>
  <p>The split is the whole mental model, and it's rigid on purpose:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The <code>.sui</code> template</strong> is markup and bindings — a
      pure read of state. It reads signals (<code>&#123;name()&#125;</code>),
      wires events (<code>on:click</code>), and binds inputs
      (<code>bind:value</code>). It holds no logic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The <code>.controller.ts</code></strong> owns the state and the
      logic. Its <code>scope()</code> returns the signals, derived values, and
      handlers the template binds to; <code>mount(state, host)</code> runs side
      effects once the element is in the DOM.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The template can only bind names the controller's <code>scope()</code> hands
    it — the compiler enforces it. Markup on one side, state on the other,
    nothing tangled between them: that boundary is why every SUI component reads
    the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>A reusable component</h2>
  <p>
    Add <code>&lt;prop&gt;</code> declarations and a scoped
    <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> and the same file compiles to a custom element you
    can drop in anywhere — exactly how the
    <a href="/components">component library</a> is built.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.component}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <p>
    deck compiles the <code>.sui</code> files, serves them, and hot-reloads on
    save. Point it at your project and open the printed URL.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.run}></sui-codeblock>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>One model, top to bottom.</strong> Pages, layouts, and library
    components are all the same <code>.sui</code> + controller pair. Learn it
    once.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/">← Overview</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/reactivity">Reactivity →</a>
  </div>
</div>
